# Text attributes for this repository, settled before the first fixture lands.
#
# Why the file exists at all. A commitment is a digest over bytes, a chain entry
# is a digest over bytes, and a vector file exists to say that a given input
# produces a given output. If a checkout adds or removes a carriage return, the
# digest changes on one operating system only, and the failure arrives as a test
# that passes for some contributors and fails for others, with nothing in the
# diff to point at.

# Everything git decides is text is stored with LF and checked out with LF, on
# every platform. Without eol=lf a checkout on Windows writes CRLF into the
# working tree, so a test that reads a tracked file back byte for byte
# disagrees with the same test on Linux while both trees are identical in git.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fixtures exist to carry exact bytes, so nothing may touch them. `binary` is
# git's own macro for `-text -diff -merge`: no end-of-line conversion in either
# direction, no textual diff, no line-level merge. The declaration is a rule on
# the path rather than a list of names somebody maintains, so a fixture added
# under this directory is covered without anybody remembering to declare it,
# and a fixture added anywhere else is not covered at all - which is what the
# `Verify fixture bytes` check refuses.
tests/fixtures/** binary

# The digest manifest sits inside the fixture directory because it describes
# it, but it is an ordinary text file rather than a fixture: it is regenerated,
# read line by line, and reviewed in a diff. The last matching line wins, so
# this one takes it back out of the rule above.
tests/fixtures/SHA256SUMS text eol=lf
